{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>Whose fat of their sacrifices they eat.</b> These are the words of the enemies, that is to say: those deities of yours who receive the offerings — let them arise and help you, for such is their faith, since they do not believe in HaShem. And it says יהי עליכם סתרה ('let it be a covering over you') in the singular and not יהיו, meaning: if you say that He is one, let Him hide you from the troubles destined to come. Or one may say that the Holy One, blessed be He, said: where are their elohim of the nations — those to whom they used to offer sacrifices and pour out wine as libations; thus did Rashi, of blessed memory, explain. And from here the Sages, of blessed memory, learned that wine of libation is forbidden for benefit, by way of the analogy of 'their wine of libation.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The closing phrase דיין נסכם appears corrupt; rendered hyper-literally as 'the analogy of their wine of libation' based on the evident reference to the hermeneutic inference (היקש) from the phrase יין נסכם in this verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:30.552682386Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}